# The record checks: what the repository must be true of ITSELF.
#
# Port of crates/heroes-cli/tests/milestones.rs, five checks that read no
# compiler output at all — they read the tree. Panel 085 named
# `milestones.rs ×5` among the tests with no successor whatever, and this
# is the module that answers it.
#
# The rule they all serve is CLAUDE.md §14: milestone identifiers are
# NAMES, not numbers, and an identifier never reaches a diagnostic. What
# the rename bought is an invariant, and without these checks it is a
# one-time cleanup that rots on the next commit.

use report
use shell
use strings

# Dated records: a panel, a journal, a measurement, a defect report, a
# story beat, a golden fixture, the design log. They KEEP the identifiers
# they were written with, and appending to one uses that file's vocabulary
# (§14) — so they are outside the walk rather than exceptions inside it.
#
# The rest are not records but noise: `vendor/` holds third-party byte
# tables whose BPE bigrams contain `M6`-shaped pairs, and
# `editors/vscode/icons/` holds SVG path data where `M14` is a moveto
# command.
#
# **`target`, `build`, `.git` and `.claude/worktrees` were on this list until
# 2026-08-19 and are not any more**, which is not an oversight: every one of them
# is gitignored, and the walk asks GIT which files are this project's rather than
# asking the filesystem what is there (`shell.project_files`). A list of
# exclusions is a premise about which junk exists — it expired the day a parallel
# session wrote a gitignored TLS key into `site/.cache/` and both record walks
# read `M3KN` out of its base64. What remains here is what git cannot know: files
# it tracks that are RECORDS.
# The one heading this project's sittings answer under. It is a CONSTANT because
# the check and the message that explains the check must not be able to disagree
# about it — the shape CLAUDE.md §11 calls a premise that expires in silence.
constant VERDICT_HEADING: str
    "\n## Author's verdict"

constant RECORD: [str]
    [
        "docs/panel"
        "docs/journal"
        "docs/measurements"
        "docs/book"
        "docs/reasoning"
        "tests/golden"
        "tests/emission"
        "DESIGN-LOG.md"
        "vendor"
        "editors/vscode/icons"
        "archive"
    ]

# **`site/log.html` was on that list and is not any more**, and it is deleted
# rather than repointed. The entry stopped matching anything the day the site's
# pages moved under `site/public/`, and it matched nothing again when they moved
# to `site/src/html/`: measured 2026-08-28, `site/log.html` selects **0** of the
# repository's tracked paths. It was also never needed. The claim, falsifiable by
# one command: **the build log names no numbered milestone in either edition** —
# measured the same day, 0 occurrences in `site/src/html/log.html` and 0 in
# `site/src/html/it/log.html`, because every entry there is written `M-<name>`.
#
# Repointing it would have restored a line that has never fired, over exactly the
# surface §14 cares most about: the site is user-visible output, and that is where
# an identifier must never appear. A dead exemption is not free — it reads as a
# statement that the log is allowed to breach the rule, and the next person to add
# an entry would believe it.

# Files git tracks that NOBODY WRITES. Not records, which are prose somebody
# meant; artifacts, whose bytes are chosen by a tool.
#
# `site/package-lock.json` is here because base64 and this check disagree about
# what a word boundary is. `opens_at` excludes a letter, a digit, `_` and `-`
# before the `M`, which is what makes `sha512-M3...` safe. It does not exclude
# `+` or `/`, and those two are IN THE BASE64 ALPHABET, so an integrity hash
# containing `+M3` or `/M9` reads as a milestone identifier. Measured 2026-08-28
# on the 232,880-byte lockfile this repository now carries: **4 occurrences**,
# `/M9`, `/M7`, `+M3` and `/M6`, every one of them inside an `integrity` field,
# and `M93B` is the one the check reported. `named` finds 0, so only this half of
# the rule is affected.
#
# The falsifiable claim: **no file in this list is written by a person, so no
# identifier in one is a claim about this project.** The day that stops being
# true of an entry, it belongs in RECORD or nowhere.
#
# Exact match, never a prefix. RECORD names trees because a record directory is
# a record all the way down; a generated file is one file, and a prefix here
# would quietly exempt a directory somebody adds later.
constant GENERATED: [str]
    [
        "site/package-lock.json"
    ]

# The blessed emissions: 5.4 MB of generated C, derived from the
# programs under `tests/golden/` and `examples/`. Reading it here
# would scan a megabyte-scale COPY of text this walk already reads
# at its source, and a milestone name in a program would be
# reported twice — once truly, once about a file nobody writes. The
# guard the walk would have given (`c_files` below) is stronger in
# `suite_emission.hero`: a `.c` in there that no program claims is
# a failure by name, which is more than "a C file lives outside the
# directories that own C".

# **The archived bootstrap is a frozen tree, not a living file.** `crates/`
# became `archive/bootstrap-rs/` at M-bootstrap-archive; nothing builds it,
# nothing runs its tests, and its 25,482 lines of Rust name every milestone
# this project ever had under the spelling it had then. Walking it would
# report five years of history as today's claims — the same argument that
# kept `.claude/worktrees` out when it was a list of exclusions: a second
# tree's names are not this tree's.

# Files whose milestone identifiers are QUOTATIONS of the retired
# spelling, kept on purpose so a test can prove the legacy form is still
# recognised. `tests/harness/` is here for the same reason the Rust file
# is: this module quotes `M0` and `M5b` in its own assertions.
constant QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING: [str]
    [
        "docs/work/DONE.md"
        "docs/ROADMAP.md"
        "CLAUDE.md"
        "tests/harness/suite_records.hero"
    ]

# Three entries left this list at M-bootstrap-archive — the bootstrap's
# `emit/tests/gate.rs`, `tests/milestones.rs` and `cli/help.rs`. They are
# inside `archive/` now, which RECORD skips whole, so an exemption for them
# would be a line that cannot fire.
#
# **The record was `docs/debrief/QUEUE.md` until 2026-08-26.** The entry is a
# SUFFIX match (`quoting` below), so the rename would have silently stopped
# matching and every `M0` and `M5b` in a 486 KB append-only record would have
# been reported as a §14 breach — the check firing loudly about the wrong
# thing, which is worse than not firing.

# The one name deliberately written without being a milestone: the
# runner-up § The names records as refused, so the reasoning survives the
# decision.
constant REFUSED: [str]
    ["M-language"]

# Measured 2026-08-25: 40 rows in the ROADMAP's alias table (39 until
# M-package-layout was scheduled — author decision 2026-08-25, `/decide`).
# The count is asserted rather than derived on purpose — it is the tripwire
# that makes a SILENTLY DROPPED row fail, which a set comparison alone
# would not. It fired on the row that added it, which is the intended
# direction: a new milestone reaches this number before it reaches a tag.
#
# **41 → 43 on 2026-09-02**, and it fired again exactly as designed: two
# milestones were scheduled in `docs/work/SCHEDULED.md` (`M-corpus-coverage`
# and `M-selfhost-nesting`, both by author instruction) and this check named
# them both as *a name with no row in the alias table* in the same run that
# filed them. A milestone reaches this number before it reaches a tag, and
# now before it reaches a step.
#
# **43 → 44 on 2026-09-02**, `M-interpolation-verdict`, scheduled by author
# instruction: a milestone whose deliverable is a RULING rather than a feature
# reaches this number the same way any other does. This one arrives before it
# has a step, a tag or a line of code, which is the direction the count was
# written for.
#
# **44 → 45 on 2026-09-03**, `M-robustness-guards`, opened by author instruction
# out of a `/decide` sitting. The sibling check `records/names` fired first, in
# the commit that scheduled six items under the name before the ROADMAP had a
# row for it — the order CLAUDE.md §14 asks for, read by an instrument.
#
# **45 → 46 on 2026-09-03**, `M-corpus-depth`, scheduled by author instruction
# from a plan measured and approved the same day, its step 0 landing with the
# row. The planning found the same milestone open in `SCHEDULED.md` and closed
# in `DESIGN-LOG.md` a day after its close — two lists disagreeing about one
# fact — which is the shape a count that must be moved by hand exists to catch.
#
# **46 → 48 on 2026-09-03**, `M-core-packages` and `M-web-framework`, scheduled
# by author instruction out of a reasoning session the same evening: two ids that
# reach this number before either has a step, a tag or a sitting — the sitting is
# at the first one's opening — as `M-interpolation-verdict` did. Two rows in one
# move because they are two deliverables (CLAUDE.md §14): the packages, and the
# framework that composes them.
#
# **48 → 55 on 2026-09-03**, later the same evening, out of a reasoning session
# that re-read the whole chain for what it lacked (`docs/reasoning/006`): four
# rulings on the language (`M-closures-verdict`, `M-reflection-verdict`,
# `M-deferral-ledger`, with `M-interpolation-verdict` moved up beside them), one
# runtime guard (`M-panic-location`), one instrument (`M-thesis-harness`), one
# tool (`M-doc-generator`) and the way in (`M-install-channels`). Seven ids that
# reach this number before any has a step, a tag or a sitting, by author
# instruction, each named for its deliverable (CLAUDE.md §14).
#
# **55 → 56 on 2026-09-03**, `M-discard-refusal`, out of the `/decide` sitting
# that ran beside M-corpus-depth step 4: `_ =` on a fallible value becomes a
# compile error (author answer `3a`). A new id rather than a step of the corpus
# milestone that found it — the instrument is `heroes mutate`'s, the deliverable
# is a language rule — and this count is what noticed the id was in
# `SCHEDULED.md` with no row here, which is this check's whole job. It went red
# twice in one minute, once for the missing row and once for this number, and
# both are the instrument working rather than failing.
constant ROWS: i64
    56

# The two documents whose citations are checked. They are the two a reader is sent
# to for a rule, and the only two this project rewrites rather than appends to.
constant WATCHED: [str]
    [
        "design.md"
        "CLAUDE.md"
    ]

# `/step`'s milestone-close number for § Where we are. A threshold with an
# instrument behind it, which is the only kind that holds.
constant WHERE_CEILING: i64
    15

# **Italian function words, and the reason the list is ASCII only.** CLAUDE.md
# §11's first line says the repository's working text is English. It said so
# while 241 Italian quotations sat in the tree, 19 of them in that very section,
# because a doctrine written at `docs/book/README.md` held that quoted speech is
# not an artifact and everything else cited it. The doctrine is retired
# (2026-09-04) and this is what replaces the honour system.
#
# The words are the ones a byte-oriented tokenizer sees: `latin_words` breaks a
# token at any byte outside `a`–`z`, and every accented character is two bytes
# above 127, so `perché` arrives as `perch` and that is the spelling listed.
# Nothing here is spelled with an accent for the same reason — `così` would
# arrive as `cos`, which is a C function, and `più` as `pi`. A marker that
# collides with English or with mathematics buys a false failure, and a check
# that cries wolf is turned off.
#
# **`un`, `chi`, `nel` and `sia` were added after the list missed a real
# sentence.** *"metti come regola che chi capita sul sito non sia un esperto"* —
# `site/CLAUDE.md`'s own instruction — reached three markers of twelve words and
# stayed under the threshold. With the four it reaches six. They were measured
# before they were added: over every tracked text file outside the records, the
# longer list fires on the same 23 declared files and nothing else.
constant ITALIAN_MARKERS: [str]
    [
        "che"
        "non"
        "sono"
        "questo"
        "questa"
        "quello"
        "quella"
        "della"
        "delle"
        "degli"
        "nella"
        "nello"
        "anche"
        "senza"
        "molto"
        "tutto"
        "tutti"
        "tutte"
        "voglio"
        "cosa"
        "quando"
        "sempre"
        "essere"
        "fare"
        "adesso"
        "ogni"
        "qualcosa"
        "niente"
        "nulla"
        "poi"
        "quindi"
        "mai"
        "solo"
        "una"
        "uno"
        "dei"
        "sul"
        "nel"
        "sia"
        "chi"
        "un"
        "alla"
        "allo"
        "agli"
        "gli"
        "perch"
    ]

# Where Italian is the DELIVERABLE rather than a leak: CLAUDE.md §11's second
# declared exception, a translation somebody reads on purpose. Prefix match, the
# same shape as `is_record`.
#
# **Two entries, and the tree that is missing from them is deliberate.** The
# Italian edition's page sources are here; its 23 `.astro` wrappers under
# `site/src/pages/it/` are NOT, and neither is `SiteNav.astro`'s table of
# Italian nav labels, because measured 2026-09-04 **not one of them fires**: a
# wrapper carries one Italian title of five or six words and this check needs
# six words on a line before it looks. A dead exemption is not free — the same
# argument that deleted `site/log.html` from RECORD above — because it reads as
# a licence, and the next person to write a paragraph of Italian into a wrapper
# would believe it had one. If that day comes the check goes red, which is the
# point, and the entry is added then with a measurement beside it.
constant ITALIAN_BY_DESIGN: [str]
    [
        "site/src/html/it"
        ".claude/skills/where/italian-vocabulary.md"
    ]

# The record that lives under no record DIRECTORY. `docs/work/DONE.md` is
# append-only and never rewritten (CLAUDE.md §3 and §14), so it keeps the words
# it was written with exactly as `docs/panel/` does — about 38 Italian quotations
# among them, left there on purpose. It is missing from `RECORD` only because
# `quoting` already takes it out of the milestone walk for an unrelated reason.
#
# **This is why `english` does not walk `living`.** `living` also drops
# `CLAUDE.md` and `docs/ROADMAP.md` — they quote the retired milestone spelling,
# so `quoting` removes them — and those two held **35 of the 55 Italian spans**
# repaired on 2026-09-04. A check reading `living` would have run green over the
# exact two files it exists for. It was written that way first, and caught
# before the commit by asking which files the walk actually hands it.
constant APPENDED: [str]
    [
        "docs/work/DONE.md"
    ]

# **The one file that must hold Italian in order to refuse it: this one.** Its
# test quotes the sentences the check has to catch, and a check that cannot
# state its own witnesses is a check nobody can read or trust. It is the same
# argument, in the same module, as `QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING` above, which is
# there because these assertions quote `M0` and `M5b`.
#
# Exact match and one entry, so the exemption cannot spread: any OTHER harness
# file that grows an Italian line is reported like anything else.
constant QUOTES_THE_ITALIAN: [str]
    [
        "tests/harness/suite_records.hero"
    ]

function run_all(scratch: str) -> report.Report?
    r: report.Report @ report.empty()

    # The quoting files leave the walk ONCE, for every check — which is
    # what the Rust `walk` does and what the first draft of this port got
    # wrong by filtering inside one check only. The three deliberate
    # misspellings `milestones.rs` quotes (to say what this check catches)
    # were reported as three real defects.
    # **Git answers which files are this project's** (shell.project_files), and
    # RECORD then removes the ones that are RECORDS. The two questions used to be
    # one `find` with a list of exclusions, and that list could not keep up with
    # the world: a gitignored TLS key in `site/.cache/` was read as `M3KN` on
    # 2026-08-19 — a milestone identifier, in a file nobody wrote and git already
    # knew to ignore.
    walked = shell.project_files(scratch)?
    living: [str] @ []

    for path in walked
        if !quoting(path) && !is_record(path) && !is_generated(path)
            living @ living.push(path)

    numbered(@r, living: living)
    named(@r, living: living)
    c_files(@r, living: living)
    citations(@r, present: walked)
    english(@r, present: walked)
    exemptions(@r, present: walked)
    expectations(@r, scratch: scratch)
    boundary(@r)
    verdicts(@r, present: walked)
    where_we_are(@r)
    return ok(r)

# Whether this path is one of the dated records — the pruning `find` used to do
# with `-not -path`, done here now that the listing comes from git. Same rule as
# the Rust twin's `rel == *r || rel.starts_with(&format!("{r}/"))`.
function is_record(path: str) -> bool
    for entry in RECORD
        if path == entry || strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: entry + "/")
            return true

    return false

# Whether this path is an artifact nobody writes. Exact match, and the reason
# it is not a prefix match like `is_record` is up at GENERATED.
function is_generated(path: str) -> bool
    for entry in GENERATED
        if path == entry
            return true

    return false

# Every exemption still selects a file.
#
# This is the check `site/log.html` needed and did not have. That entry stopped
# matching anything the day the site's pages moved into `site/public/`, and it
# went on sitting in RECORD reading like a rule — the walk skipped nothing, and
# nothing said so. The comment above QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING describes the same
# accident from the other side: a record was renamed, its SUFFIX match silently
# stopped firing, and 486 KB of legacy identifiers would have been reported as
# breaches. Both are one failure, an exemption that no longer reaches anything,
# and it is invisible in both directions — the list still reads as correct.
#
# The three lists match differently and the question is the same for all three:
# does this line still reach a file git tracks? RECORD names trees, GENERATED
# names one file exactly, QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING matches a suffix.
function exemptions(@r: report.Report, present: [str])
    dead: [str] @ []

    for entry in RECORD
        if !selects_tree(entry: entry, present: present)
            dead @ dead.push("    RECORD: " + entry)

    for entry in GENERATED
        if !selects_exactly(entry: entry, present: present)
            dead @ dead.push("    GENERATED: " + entry)

    for entry in QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING
        if !selects_suffix(entry: entry, present: present)
            dead @ dead.push("    QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING: " + entry)

    if dead.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/exemptions",
            detail: "  an exemption selects no file this repository tracks:\n" + dead.join("\n") + "\n  Either the path moved, and the entry has to follow it, or nothing needs the\n  exemption any more and the line comes out. A rule that reaches nothing still\n  reads as a rule to whoever finds it next."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

function selects_tree(entry: str, present: [str]) -> bool
    for path in present
        if path == entry || strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: entry + "/")
            return true

    return false

function selects_exactly(entry: str, present: [str]) -> bool
    for path in present
        if path == entry
            return true

    return false

function selects_suffix(entry: str, present: [str]) -> bool
    for path in present
        if strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: entry)
            return true

    return false

# No living file names a numbered milestone.
#
# If this fires on a file you just wrote: the identifier is a name now —
# the map is `docs/ROADMAP.md` § The names and the algorithm is CLAUDE.md
# §14. If the file is a dated record, add it to RECORD and say why in the
# commit.
function numbered(@r: report.Report, living: [str])
    offenders: [str] @ []

    for path in living
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            continue
        found = numbered_in(text.must())

        if !found.is_err()
            offenders @ offenders.push("    " + path + ": " + found.must())

    if offenders.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/numbered",
            detail: "  a living file names a numbered milestone — §14 says identifiers are names:\n" + offenders.join("\n")
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# Every `M-<name>` in use has a row in the ROADMAP's alias table, and the
# table has the number of rows it had. The map is parsed out of the
# ROADMAP so the table cannot drift from the tree in EITHER direction: a
# new milestone with no row fails here, and so does a row nobody uses
# being the only place a name exists.
function named(@r: report.Report, living: [str])
    table = alias_table()

    if table.len() != ROWS
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/table",
            detail: "  the alias table has " + table.len().to_str() + " rows, not " + ROWS.to_str()
        )
    known: {str: bool} @ {}

    for row in table
        known[row] @ true

    for name in REFUSED
        known[name] @ true

    unknown: [str] @ []

    for path in living
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            continue

        for name in names_used(text.must())
            if known[name].is_err()
                unknown @ unknown.push("    " + path + ": " + name)

    if unknown.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/names",
            detail: "  a name with no row in the alias table:\n" + unknown.join("\n")
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# No C file lives outside the directories that own C.
#
# This exists because a throwaway that measured `__builtin_classify_type`
# was left in the repository root and `git add -A` committed and pushed it
# (2026-08-14). The list is of DIRECTORIES that own C, not of file names:
# a new runtime part or a fifth spike needs no edit here.
function c_files(@r: report.Report, living: [str])
    # No leading `./`: `git ls-files` spells a path from the repository root,
    # where `find .` spelled it from the current directory.
    owns: [str] @ ["runtime/", "tools/spike/", "seed/"]
    strays: [str] @ []

    for path in living
        if !strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".c") && !strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".h")
            continue
        owned: bool @ false

        for dir in owns
            if strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: dir)
                owned @ true

        if !owned
            strays @ strays.push("    " + path)

    if strays.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/c",
            detail: "  a C file outside the directories that own C:\n" + strays.join("\n")
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# The two living documents may not cite a path that does not exist.
#
# **This is the check Linux had to build.** `make refcheckdocs`
# (`scripts/documentation-file-ref-check`) exists because live documentation kept
# citing paths that had moved — a 27-patch *"docs: Fix more broken references"*
# series in 2018 — and panel 086's historian named it as the documented failure
# mode of leaving a citation pointing into an archive. This project was one commit
# away from the same thing: `design.md` cited
# `crates/heroes/src/measure/gate.rs` twice as a LIVE home while `crates/` was
# being archived.
#
# THE RULE IS ANCHORED, AND THE ALTERNATIVE WAS MEASURED AND REFUSED. Panel 086's
# compiler-engineer measured both: "any backticked token that looks like a path"
# gives **145 occurrences, 84 distinct, 44 of them missing — 52% false positives**,
# which is a check nobody would keep. Anchored — the token's FIRST component is an
# existing directory of this repository, and the token holds no `*`, `<`, `>`, `{`,
# `}` or space — gives **47 occurrences, 34 distinct, 2 misses**, and both misses
# are directory prefixes without a slug (`docs/panel/042`), which prefix matching
# accepts with no allow-list at all.
#
# Its honest limit, stated by the seat that measured it: it catches four of the six
# citations in panel 086's own class. `CLAUDE.md`'s `{lexer,syntax,types}` brace
# form and design.md's quoted `grep` command are outside the rule by construction —
# a rule that tried to read those would be the 52% one.
function citations(@r: report.Report, present: [str])
    known: {str: bool} @ {}
    roots: {str: bool} @ {}

    for path in present
        known[path] @ true
        at = strings.index_of(text: path, needle: "/")

        if !at.is_err()
            roots[path.slice(from: 0, to: at.must())] @ true

    dead: [str] @ []

    for document in WATCHED
        text = read_file(document)

        if text.is_err()
            report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/citations", detail: "  cannot read " + document)
            return

        for token in path_tokens(text: text.must(), roots: roots)
            if !exists_or_prefixes(token: token, known: known)
                dead @ dead.push("    " + document + ": " + token)

    if dead.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/citations",
            detail: "  a living document cites a path that does not exist:\n" + dead.join("\n") + "\n  A citation into a moved or deleted file is read as current by whoever follows\n  it. If the file moved, repoint the sentence; if the claim is dated history,\n  say so in the sentence and point at where the bytes are now."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# **The repository's working text is English** (CLAUDE.md §11), and this is the
# check that says so out loud instead of trusting whoever writes next.
#
# It reads every file that is not a record — the records keep the words they were
# written with (§14) — so a red line here is never history: it is a rule, a
# comment or a page that somebody wrote in Italian today. It takes the whole
# walk and does its own pruning rather than the `living` list, and `APPENDED`
# above says why in full: `living` drops the two files this rule exists for.
#
# **Why per line and not per file.** The shape this catches is one Italian
# sentence inside an English document: a quoted author instruction, a module doc
# citing one, a gloss nobody translated. A file-wide ratio drowns one sentence in
# four hundred English ones, which is exactly the case that got past every reader
# for a month.
function english(@r: report.Report, present: [str])
    found: [str] @ []

    for path in present
        if is_record(path) || is_generated(path) || is_appended(path)
            continue

        if quotes_the_italian(path)
            continue

        if !reads_as_prose(path) || italian_by_design(path)
            continue

        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            continue

        for line in strings.lines(text.must())
            if italian_line(line)
                found @ found.push("    " + path + ": " + strings.trimmed(line))

    if found.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/english",
            detail: "  a living file is written in Italian:\n" + found.join("\n") + "\n  CLAUDE.md §11: everything written is English, and that binds a quoted author\n  instruction too — it is written down for what it MEANT, in English, and the\n  original stays in the git history and in the dated records. If this text is a\n  translation somebody reads on purpose, it belongs under §11's second declared\n  exception and its path goes in ITALIAN_BY_DESIGN with a measurement beside it."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# Whether this file carries prose a human wrote. An extension list rather than a
# guess at the content: a `.json` lockfile and a `.svg` icon hold neither
# English nor Italian, and reading them buys only the chance of a false word.
function reads_as_prose(path: str) -> bool
    for suffix in [".md", ".hero", ".c", ".h", ".html", ".astro", ".txt"]
        if strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: suffix)
            return true

    return false

# Prefix match, so a directory covers the files under it — `is_record`'s rule,
# and the `/` guard is why `site/src/html/italy.md` would not be exempted by an
# entry naming `site/src/html/it`.
# This module itself, by exact name. A suffix match like `quoting`'s would also
# exempt a copy of this file anywhere in the tree; there is one.
function quotes_the_italian(path: str) -> bool
    for entry in QUOTES_THE_ITALIAN
        if path == entry
            return true

    return false

# The record with no directory of its own, kept out by exact name.
function is_appended(path: str) -> bool
    for entry in APPENDED
        if path == entry
            return true

    return false

function italian_by_design(path: str) -> bool
    for entry in ITALIAN_BY_DESIGN
        if path == entry || strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: entry + "/")
            return true

    return false

function is_marker(word: str) -> bool
    for marker in ITALIAN_MARKERS
        if same_folded(word: word, marker: marker)
            return true

    return false

# ASCII case-folded equality. The fold happens HERE rather than while the word
# is being cut, because cutting builds a word out of slices of the line and a
# slice carries the byte it found: lowercasing at comparison time is the one
# place where the byte is a value and not a position. `Non` and `non` are the
# same marker; the markers themselves are all lowercase.
function same_folded(word: str, marker: str) -> bool
    if word.len() != marker.len()
        return false

    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < word.len()
        b: u8 @ word[i]

        if b >= 65 && b <= 90
            b @ b + 32

        if b != marker[i]
            return false
        i @ i + 1

    return true

# The line's words, split at every byte that is not an ASCII letter. Case is
# left alone here and folded by `same_folded` when a word is compared.
#
# **The inline code spans go first**, before a single word is counted, because
# `site/CLAUDE.md`'s Italian spelling rule is a row of backticked Italian words
# — `dai`, `perch`, `po'` — being ruled ON rather than written IN, and it is the
# one line in the repository that would fire while obeying every rule in it.
# Stripping code spans also drops identifiers, paths and emitted C, none of
# which is prose in any language.
function latin_words(line: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []
    piece: [str] @ []
    inside_code: bool @ false
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < line.len()
        byte = line[i]

        if byte == 96
            inside_code @ !inside_code

            if piece.len() > 0
                out @ out.push(piece.join(""))
                piece @ []
            i @ i + 1
            continue

        letter: bool @ false

        if !inside_code
            if is_lower(byte) || byte >= 65 && byte <= 90
                piece @ piece.push(line.slice(from: i, to: i + 1))
                letter @ true

        if !letter && piece.len() > 0
            out @ out.push(piece.join(""))
            piece @ []
        i @ i + 1

    if piece.len() > 0
        out @ out.push(piece.join(""))

    return out

# Every backticked token that is anchored at a directory of this repository.
function path_tokens(text: str, roots: {str: bool}) -> [str]
    found: [str] @ []
    inside: bool @ false
    piece: [str] @ []

    for ch in text.chars()
        if ch == "`"
            if inside
                token = without_member(piece.join(""))

                if anchored(token: token, roots: roots)
                    found @ found.push(token)
                piece @ piece.slice(from: 0, to: 0)
            inside @ !inside
        else if inside
            piece @ piece.push(ch)

    return found

# A citation may name what is IN the file — `selfhost/cli/flags.hero::flags()` —
# and the path claim is the half before the `::`. Cutting it is not cosmetic:
# **it is what would have caught the citation that provoked this** (panel 092).
# `CLAUDE.md` cited `commands/flags.rs::FLAGS` for months and this check never
# looked at it, because `anchored` asks whether the first component is a
# directory this repository has (`commands` is not) OR whether the token ends in
# a file extension (`::FLAGS` is not one). Cut the member and the same token ends
# in `.rs`, is anchored by its extension, and is reported dead. Found by a panel
# judge with no `grep`, reading the contract as a stranger would — which is the
# argument for the cut rather than for a wider net.
function without_member(token: str) -> str
    at = strings.index_of(text: token, needle: "::")

    if at.is_err()
        return token
    return token.slice(from: 0, to: at.must())

# Whether this backticked token is a path claim: one word, no glob or brace, and
# then EITHER its first component is a directory this repository has, OR it ends in
# a file extension.
#
# **The second half is not belt-and-braces, it is the hole the first half had.**
# Anchoring on existing directories is self-adjusting in the wrong direction: the
# moment `crates/` moved to `archive/bootstrap-rs/`, every citation of
# `crates/heroes/src/measure/gate.rs` stopped being a path claim — because its root
# had ceased to exist — so the check went green over exactly the citations it was
# built to catch. Measured 2026-08-19, one commit after it landed: 6 passed, three
# dead citations unseen. A file extension is a fact about the token itself and
# survives the deletion of everything around it.
function anchored(token: str, roots: {str: bool}) -> bool
    at = strings.index_of(text: token, needle: "/")

    if at.is_err()
        return false

    for ch in token.chars()
        if ch == " " || ch == "*" || ch == "<" || ch == ">" || ch == "{" || ch == "}" || ch == "\"" || ch == "`"
            return false

    if !roots[token.slice(from: 0, to: at.must())].is_err()
        return true
    return has_extension(token)

# Whether the token's last component carries a `.suffix` of one to nine letters or
# digits — `gate.rs`, `ci.yml`, `heroes-spec.md`. Not `docs/panel/042`, and not
# `application/json`.
function has_extension(token: str) -> bool
    cs = token.chars()
    dot: i64 @ -1
    slash: i64 @ -1
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < cs.len()
        if cs[i] == "."
            dot @ i

        if cs[i] == "/"
            slash @ i
        i @ i + 1

    if dot <= slash + 1 || dot == cs.len() - 1
        return false

    if cs.len() - 1 - dot > 9
        return false
    j: i64 @ dot + 1

    while j < cs.len()
        if cs[j].len() != 1 || !is_lower(cs[j][0]) && !is_digit(cs[j][0])
            return false
        j @ j + 1

    return true

# A token names something if a file IS it, or if a file begins with it — which
# covers a directory (`tests/golden/check/`) and a record cited by its number
# without its slug (`docs/panel/042`).
function exists_or_prefixes(token: str, known: {str: bool}) -> bool
    if !known[token].is_err()
        return true
    trimmed = strings.without_suffix(text: token, suffix: "/")

    for path in keys(known)
        if strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: trimmed)
            return true

    return false

# No golden's expected output names a milestone in EITHER spelling.
#
# The byte-pinned diagnostic text is where a leak from any diagnostic
# would surface — not only from the emit gate, which is the only one with
# a guard of its own. §4.17's standard: everything needed without opening
# another file, and a milestone identifier is a pointer into a file the
# reader does not have.
function expectations(@r: report.Report, scratch: str)
    none: [str] = []
    listing = shell.list_tree(scratch: scratch, dir: "tests/golden", skip: none)

    if listing.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/expected", detail: "  cannot walk tests/golden")
        return
    offenders: [str] @ []

    for path in listing.must()
        if !strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".expected")
            continue
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            continue
        found = numbered_in(text.must())

        if !found.is_err()
            offenders @ offenders.push("    " + path + ": " + found.must())

        for name in names_used(text.must())
            offenders @ offenders.push("    " + path + ": " + name)

    if offenders.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/expected",
            detail: "  a pinned diagnostic names a milestone:\n" + offenders.join("\n")
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# The boundary rule, asserted in both directions — the check that keeps
# the two checks above honest.
# EVERY SITTING CARRIES ITS AUTHOR'S VERDICT, AND IT DOES NOT SAY `Pending`.
#
# CLAUDE.md §4 says a panel never blocks: it adopts a conservative default,
# ships it, and queues the author's ratification. That contract has one hole and
# it is not in the rule — it is that NOTHING ASKS. Panels 085, 086 and 087 sat
# unratifiable for four days because no list carried the item; panels 069 to 079
# sat for eight, eleven of them, and were found by accident on 2026-08-24 while
# ratifying three others.
#
# **WHY THIS CHECK ASKS THE VALUE AND NOT THE VOCABULARY** (CLAUDE.md §11's own
# distinction, and this is the measurement that proves it). Five attempts to
# count the unratified by hand that day returned 51, 7, 16, 12 and 11, every one
# wrong differently:
#   - `## Author's verdict` was a recent convention, so old sittings ratified by
#     a DESIGN-LOG line looked unratified;
#   - **the phrase `ratification pending` CONTAINS the string `ratif`**, so a
#     grep for *ratified* counted every unratified sitting as ratified;
#   - `docs/panel/080` says *"no ratification pending"* and matched as pending,
#     its own negation read as the thing it denies;
#   - and a yes is written as *ratified*, *author decision*, *author instruction*
#     or *decided by the author*, across four different files.
# Every one of those failures is a premise about the WORLD — about which words
# the record happens to use. This check rests on a fact about the FILE IN HAND:
# it has exactly one verdict section, and that section does not open with
# `Pending`. A vocabulary can drift; a section either exists or it does not.
#
# The rule has NO EXCEPTION, deliberately. `080` is a retro-record settled before
# its own sitting, and rather than teach the check about retro-records it was
# given a verdict section saying so — a rule with an exception is a rule the next
# exception breaks.
function verdicts(@r: report.Report, present: [str])
    offenders: [str] @ []

    for path in present
        if !is_sitting(path)
            continue

        if sitting_number(path).default(0) < VERDICT_FLOOR
            continue
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/verdicts", detail: "  cannot read " + path)
            return
        pieces = strings.split_text(text: text.must(), separator: VERDICT_HEADING)

        if pieces.len() != 2
            offenders @ offenders.push(
                "    " + path + ": " + (pieces.len() - 1).to_str() + " verdict sections, want exactly 1"
            )
            continue

        if opens_pending(text: pieces[1]) && !queued(path: path)
            offenders @ offenders.push(
                "    " + path + ": the verdict reads `Pending` and no open item in " + DECISIONS + " names it"
            )

    if offenders.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/verdicts",
            detail: "  a sitting has no answer from the author:\n" + offenders.join("\n") + "\n  CLAUDE.md §4: a panel never blocks, it queues the author's ratification — so\n  something has to ask. Give the file one `" + VERDICT_HEADING.slice(from: 1, to: VERDICT_HEADING.len()) + "` section saying what the yes\n  settles and what it does not. A sitting settled BEFORE it ran is a retro-record\n  and says so under the same heading; there is no exception to teach this check."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# WHERE THE RULE STARTS, AND WHY IT IS A NUMBER.
#
# Measured 2026-08-24, on the check's first run: **57 sittings below 063 carry no
# verdict section at all**, and every one of them is nonetheless RATIFIED — by a
# `DESIGN-LOG.md` line, by `docs/panel/OPEN-QUESTIONS.md`, or by a blanket author
# instruction. They predate the convention rather than break it. CLAUDE.md §14
# says a dated record is not rewritten, so this check does not demand that 57
# files be retrofitted to satisfy an instrument invented after them.
#
# 063 is the floor because it is exact rather than round: every sitting from 063
# up carries a verdict, and no sitting below it does. If that ever stops being
# true the floor moves DOWN with a line saying which sitting moved it — never up,
# because raising it would hide the thing the check exists to find.
constant VERDICT_FLOOR: i64
    63

# The sitting's number, or an error where the name does not start with one.
function sitting_number(path: str) -> i64?
    rest = strings.without_prefix(text: path, prefix: "docs/panel/")
    at = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: "-")

    if at.is_err()
        return fail("unnumbered", "no `-` after the number in " + path)
    return strings.to_number(text: rest.slice(from: 0, to: at.must()))

# **A PENDING VERDICT IS FINE — A PENDING VERDICT NOBODY IS ASKING ABOUT IS NOT**,
# and the difference is the whole defect this check was built for (corrected
# 2026-08-25, hours after the check landed, by the check firing on the panel held
# that evening).
#
# The first version failed on any `Pending`, which made every freshly-held sitting
# a red build until the author answered — and CLAUDE.md §4 says in its own words
# that **a panel never blocks**: it adopts a conservative default, ships it, and
# QUEUES the ratification. A check that contradicts the contract it enforces is
# worse than no check.
#
# What actually went wrong with panels 085-087, and with 069-079 for eight days,
# was never that they were pending. It was that **no list carried the item**, so
# nothing asked. So that is what this asks: a sitting may say `Pending` for as
# long as it likes, provided an OPEN item in `docs/work/DECIDE.md` names it.
# The pair is the invariant; neither half alone is.
#
# **The constant was called `QUEUE` and held `docs/debrief/QUEUE.md`'s neighbour
# until 2026-08-26**, which is the confusion the rename fixed one level up: it
# reads the DECISIONS list, and calling it the queue is what let `/panel`'s own
# skill file tell a sitting to append its ratification item to the record instead
# — to the one file this function never opens. A miss here is silent in the worst
# direction: `read_file` fails, `queued` returns false for every sitting, and
# every pending panel is reported as unqueued.
constant DECISIONS: str
    "docs/work/DECIDE.md"

# Does an OPEN item in the queue name this sitting? The number is the key, because
# a panel file's name changes and its number does not (CLAUDE.md §14: an id is
# never renamed once it is in the record).
#
# **THE DIGITS AS THE FILENAME SPELLS THEM, NOT THE INTEGER THEY PARSE TO**
# (repaired 2026-08-25, by this check firing on panel 091 — the first sitting to
# reach it with a pending verdict). It read `"panel " + number.to_str()`, so for
# `091-*.md` it hunted for `panel 91` while every reference in this repository
# writes `panel 091`: measured that day, `docs/debrief/DECIDE.md` contains the
# padded form 30-odd times and the unpadded form **zero** times. So `queued`
# could not return true for any sitting between 063 and 099 — the entire live
# range — and the correction that gave this check its second half was inert from
# the hour it landed. It never showed because the only pending sitting since was
# ratified the same night.
#
# CLAUDE.md §11's rule, in one line: the file's own digits are a fact about the
# VALUE IN HAND; how the repository happens to spell a number is a premise about
# the world, and it expired silently. Both forms are accepted because a human
# writing the item may reach for either and neither can name a different sitting.
function queued(path: str) -> bool
    digits = sitting_digits(path)

    if digits.is_err()
        return false
    text = read_file(DECISIONS)

    if text.is_err()
        return false
    padded = "panel " + digits.must()
    bare: str @ padded
    number = strings.to_number(text: digits.must())

    if !number.is_err()
        bare @ "panel " + number.must().to_str()

    for line in strings.lines(text: text.must())
        if strings.starts_with(text: line, prefix: "- [ ] ")
            if strings.contains(text: line, needle: padded) || strings.contains(text: line, needle: bare)
                return true

    return false

# The digits a sitting's filename opens with, exactly as written. Kept apart from
# `sitting_number` because the two answer different questions: the floor needs an
# integer to compare, and the queue needs the spelling the record uses.
function sitting_digits(path: str) -> str?
    rest = strings.without_prefix(text: path, prefix: "docs/panel/")
    at = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: "-")

    if at.is_err()
        return fail("unnumbered", "no `-` after the number in " + path)
    return ok(rest.slice(from: 0, to: at.must()))

# A numbered sitting under `docs/panel/`. `OPEN-QUESTIONS.md` is not one: it is
# the watch list, it has no resolution, and nobody ratifies it.
function is_sitting(path: str) -> bool
    if !strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: "docs/panel/")
        return false

    if !strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".md")
        return false
    rest = path.slice(from: "docs/panel/".len(), to: path.len())

    if rest.len() == 0
        return false

    # The BYTE, not a one-character slice: `<` and friends take a number in this
    # language and never a `str` (spec § Operators), which is the first thing
    # this function got wrong.
    first = rest[0]
    return first >= '0' && first <= '9'

# Does this section OPEN with the stub? Only the first non-empty line is asked,
# and the emphasis markers are stepped over, because a real verdict may quote the
# word `pending` while saying what it settles — 089's does, twice. Asking the
# first line asks whether anybody answered; asking the whole section would ask
# what they wrote about, which is a different question and not this one.
function opens_pending(text: str) -> bool
    for line in strings.lines(text: text)
        bare: str @ ""

        for ch in line.chars()
            if ch != "*" && ch != " " && ch != "\t" && ch != "_"
                bare @ bare + ch

        if bare.len() == 0
            continue
        return strings.starts_with(text: bare, prefix: "Pending")

    return true

# **`docs/ROADMAP.md` § Where we are, held to its own ceiling.**
#
# The rule is `/step`'s milestone-close checklist: that section says what is
# NEXT, and a closed milestone's record is its journal, so the section stays at
# 15 lines. It was written with its reason — the file reached 935 lines, growing
# ~66 per close, and a reader met 512 lines about the past before the first line
# about the future.
#
# **It had no executor, and it was broken by 16 lines.** Measured 2026-08-31 at
# M-argv-execution: 31 lines, two long paragraphs of re-measurement history that
# belong to the journals. CLAUDE.md §3 names this exact shape — a rule written in
# three documents that no skill performed, which is how `DECIDE.md` grew to
# 391 KB. A number in a checklist is a wish; a number an instrument reads is a
# rule.
#
# The count is every line from the heading to the `---` that ends the section,
# blank lines included, because a blank line costs a reader the same scroll as
# any other.
function where_we_are(@r: report.Report)
    text = read_file("docs/ROADMAP.md")

    if text.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/roadmap", detail: "  docs/ROADMAP.md cannot be read")
        return
    counted: i64 @ 0
    inside: bool @ false
    closed: bool @ false

    for row in strings.lines(text.must())
        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "## Where we are")
            inside @ true

        if inside && !closed
            if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "---")
                closed @ true
            else
                counted @ counted + 1

    if !inside
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/roadmap",
            detail: "  docs/ROADMAP.md has no `## Where we are` section, and /step's checklist names one"
        )
        return

    if counted > WHERE_CEILING
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/roadmap",
            detail: "  § Where we are is " + counted.to_str() + " lines and the ceiling is " + WHERE_CEILING.to_str() + " — the ROADMAP says what is NEXT, and a closed milestone's record is its journal (/step, milestone close)"
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

function boundary(@r: report.Report)
    caught: [str] @ ["scheduled for M8c", "at M5b the increfs", "M0 was day zero", "(M6+)"]
    clean: [str] @ [
        "STAC-M3 Antuco benchmarks"
        "INT64_MIN % -1 does not trap on ARM64"
        "the RM64 encoding"
        "M-selfhost-fixpoint is v1"
        "hero_desc_str"
    ]
    wrong: [str] @ []

    for text in caught
        if numbered_in(text).is_err()
            wrong @ wrong.push("    not caught: " + text)

    for text in clean
        if !numbered_in(text).is_err()
            wrong @ wrong.push("    false positive: " + text)

    if wrong.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/boundary", detail: "  the boundary rule is wrong:\n" + wrong.join("\n"))
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# An `M` followed by a digit at a word boundary — nothing alphanumeric,
# `_` or `-` before the `M`, so `ARM64` is not a milestone and neither is
# `STAC-M3`, kdb+'s benchmark suite: the one genuine false positive a
# word-anchored search hits, and the reason the rule looks behind.
function numbered_in(text: str) -> str?
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < text.len()
        if text[i] == 77 && opens_at(text: text, at: i) && i + 1 < text.len() && is_digit(text[i + 1])
            # **Four bytes AT MOST, and never across a character.** `slice`
            # aborts when it splits one (spec:148), and the Rust twin cannot:
            # it reaches for `String::from_utf8_lossy`, which replaces a broken
            # byte instead of stopping. Heroes has no lossy conversion, so the
            # window stops at the first byte above ASCII — every byte in the
            # answer is then one character wide. Found 2026-08-19 the first time
            # this walk read a file with an em dash three bytes after an `M8a`.
            last: i64 @ i + 1

            while last < text.len() && last < i + 4 && text[last] < 128
                last @ last + 1

            return ok(text.slice(from: i, to: last))
        i @ i + 1

    return fail("none", "no numbered milestone")

# Every `M-<name>` in the text, with a trailing hyphen left to the prose.
function names_used(text: str) -> [str]
    found: [str] @ []
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < text.len()
        if text[i] == 77 && opens_at(text: text, at: i) && i + 2 < text.len() && text[i + 1] == 45 && is_lower(text[i + 2])
            last: i64 @ i + 2

            while last < text.len() && (is_lower(text[last]) || text[last] == 45)
                last @ last + 1

            while last > i + 2 && text[last - 1] == 45
                last @ last - 1

            found @ found.push(text.slice(from: i, to: last))
            i @ last
        else
            i @ i + 1

    return found

# Whether this file is one that quotes the retired spelling on purpose.
function quoting(path: str) -> bool
    for name in QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING
        if strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: name)
            return true

    return false

function opens_at(text: str, at: i64) -> bool
    if at == 0
        return true
    before = text[at - 1]

    if is_lower(before) || is_digit(before)
        return false

    if before >= 65 && before <= 90
        return false
    return before != 95 && before != 45

function is_digit(byte: u8) -> bool
    return byte >= 48 && byte <= 57

function is_lower(byte: u8) -> bool
    return byte >= 97 && byte <= 122

# The names the ROADMAP's alias table carries: every row opening with
# "| `M-".
function alias_table() -> [str]
    text = read_file("docs/ROADMAP.md")

    if text.is_err()
        none: [str] = []
        return none
    rows: [str] @ []

    for row in strings.lines(text.must())
        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "| `M-")
            rest = strings.without_prefix(text: row, prefix: "| `")
            at = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: "`")

            if !at.is_err()
                rows @ rows.push(rest.slice(from: 0, to: at.must()))

    return rows

test "a dead citation is caught, and the anchored rule ignores what it must"
    roots: {str: bool} @ {}
    roots["docs"] @ true
    roots["selfhost"] @ true
    known: {str: bool} @ {}
    known["docs/ROADMAP.md"] @ true
    known["docs/panel/086-the-ledger-that-outlives-its-file.md"] @ true
    known["selfhost/main.hero"] @ true

    # A path claim that holds, one that does not.
    assert exists_or_prefixes(token: "docs/ROADMAP.md", known: known)
    assert !exists_or_prefixes(token: "docs/NOSUCH.md", known: known)

    # A record cited by its number, without its slug — the case that would need an
    # allow-list under a stricter rule.
    assert exists_or_prefixes(token: "docs/panel/086", known: known)

    # A directory, with and without its slash.
    assert exists_or_prefixes(token: "selfhost/", known: known)

    # What the rule must NOT read as a path claim: a brace form, a glob, a
    # command with spaces, a bare word, and something rooted nowhere.
    assert !anchored(token: "docs/heroes/src/{lexer,syntax}/", roots: roots)
    assert !anchored(token: "docs/**/*.md", roots: roots)
    assert !anchored(token: "grep -rn ORDER: selfhost/", roots: roots)
    assert !anchored(token: "SPEC_TOKENS", roots: roots)
    assert !anchored(token: "vendor/tokenizers", roots: roots)

    # And what it must read: an ordinary citation.
    assert anchored(token: "docs/ROADMAP.md", roots: roots)
    assert anchored(token: "selfhost/main.hero", roots: roots)

    # The tokens come out of the backticks and nowhere else.
    tokens = path_tokens(
        text: "prose selfhost/main.hero is not in backticks, `selfhost/main.hero` is, and `plain` is not a path",
        roots: roots
    )
    assert tokens == ["selfhost/main.hero"]

    # The check itself, firing: a stand-in document citing something absent.
    fired: report.Report @ report.empty()
    citations(@fired, present: ["design.md"])
    assert fired.failed + fired.passed == 1

test "the boundary rule catches the numbers and nothing else"
    assert !numbered_in("scheduled for M8c").is_err()
    assert !numbered_in("at M5b the increfs").is_err()
    assert !numbered_in("M0 was day zero").is_err()
    assert !numbered_in("(M6+)").is_err()
    assert numbered_in("STAC-M3 Antuco benchmarks").is_err()
    assert numbered_in("INT64_MIN % -1 does not trap on ARM64").is_err()
    assert numbered_in("the RM64 encoding").is_err()
    assert numbered_in("M-selfhost-fixpoint is v1").is_err()
    assert numbered_in("hero_desc_str").is_err()

# The check that CLAUDE.md §11 is obeyed, made to fire on purpose (§9: every
# verifier check has a test that makes it fire). The Italian line is the shape
# that got past every reader for a month — one quoted instruction inside an
# English document — and the English lines are the three that would have made
# this check cry wolf: a `constant` full of identifiers, a sentence about the
# Italian edition, and the spelling rule whose subject is Italian words.
test "an Italian line is caught, and the English ones around it are not"
    assert italian_line("non voglio nulla in memoria e voglio tutto nel Claude MD")
    assert italian_line("metti come regola che chi capita sul sito non sia un esperto")
    assert italian_line("Non le voglio le date, non le voglio assolutamente")
    assert !italian_line("Italian nav labels are wider, plus the language badge, so the")
    assert !italian_line("never `dai`; `perche`, `finche`, `trentatre` with the acute accent")
    assert !italian_line("the author studies from the Italian, so where the two diverge")
    assert !italian_line("a b c d e")

    assert italian_by_design("site/src/html/it/index.html")
    assert italian_by_design(".claude/skills/where/italian-vocabulary.md")
    assert !italian_by_design("site/src/html/index.html")
    assert !italian_by_design("CLAUDE.md")

    assert reads_as_prose("CLAUDE.md")
    assert reads_as_prose("selfhost/grammar_expr.hero")
    assert !reads_as_prose("site/package-lock.json")

    assert latin_words("due, tre cose").len() == 3
    assert latin_words("a `token dropped` b").len() == 2
    assert same_folded(word: "Non", marker: "non")
    assert !same_folded(word: "none", marker: "non")

# **Four markers AND a quarter of the line.** Both, because a long English line
# reaches four markers on its own and a short one reaches a quarter on `una`
# alone. Six words before the line is looked at at all, so a heading, a table
# cell and a nav label are below the horizon.
#
# Measured 2026-09-04 over every tracked `.md`, `.hero`, `.c`, `.h`, `.html`,
# `.astro` and `.txt` file outside the records: **23 files fire, and all 23 are
# declared** — the Italian edition's 22 pages and the `/where` vocabulary. Zero
# false positives, which is the number this threshold was chosen to hit.
function italian_line(line: str) -> bool
    words = latin_words(line)

    if words.len() < 6
        return false

    marked: i64 @ 0

    for word in words
        if is_marker(word)
            marked @ marked + 1

    return marked >= 4 && marked * 100 >= 25 * words.len()

test "names_used reads a name and leaves the prose alone"
    assert names_used("M-harness-port is next") == ["M-harness-port"]
    assert names_used("see M-selfhost-fixpoint — and M-bootstrap-archive").len() == 2

    # A trailing hyphen belongs to the prose, not to the name.
    assert names_used("the M-harness-port- dash")[0] == "M-harness-port"
    assert names_used("ARM-based").len() == 0
    assert names_used("no names here").len() == 0

test "the alias table is read out of the ROADMAP"
    table = alias_table()
    assert table.len() == ROWS
    assert table[0].len() > 2

test "a sitting's queue item is found by the digits the record actually writes"
    # The defect this repaired: `091-*.md` parsed to 91 and hunted for
    # `panel 91`, while every item in the queue writes `panel 091`. The
    # padded form is what the repository uses; the bare one is accepted
    # because a human may reach for it and it names no other sitting.
    assert sitting_digits("docs/panel/091-a-name.md").must() == "091"
    assert sitting_digits("docs/panel/007-a-name.md").must() == "007"
    assert sitting_digits("docs/panel/OPEN-QUESTIONS.md").must() == "OPEN"
    assert sitting_digits("docs/panel/nodash.md").is_err()

    # The integer is still what the floor compares, and the two answers
    # differ exactly where the defect lived.
    assert sitting_number("docs/panel/091-a-name.md").must() == 91
    assert sitting_digits("docs/panel/091-a-name.md").must() != sitting_number("docs/panel/091-a-name.md").must().to_str()

test "the pending rule fires only when nothing is asking"
    # ONE DIRECTION ONLY, AND THE MISSING ONE IS THE LESSON. This case asserted
    # `queued("091-…")` when it was written on 2026-08-25, because panel 091 was
    # pending and its item was open. Both sittings were ratified four hours
    # later, `DECIDE.md` went to zero, and the assertion went red — a premise
    # about the WORLD (what the queue happens to hold) inside a test, which is
    # CLAUDE.md §11's exact shape, written by the person whose job is to police
    # it. What survives is the half that rests on the value in hand: a sitting
    # number nobody has ever held is queued by nothing, whatever the queue says
    # today. The positive direction is exercised on every run by the check
    # itself — it is what refused `docs/panel/091` and `092` until each had an
    # item, which is how the `panel 91` vs `panel 091` defect above was found.
    assert !queued(path: "docs/panel/999-a-sitting-nobody-held.md")

test "a citation that names a member is checked at its file, and that is the hole it closes"
    # The token that provoked panel 092: `CLAUDE.md` carried it for months and
    # this check never looked, because neither half of `anchored` fired — the
    # first component is not a repo directory and `::FLAGS` is not an extension.
    assert without_member("commands/flags.rs::FLAGS") == "commands/flags.rs"
    assert without_member("selfhost/cli/flags.hero::flags()") == "selfhost/cli/flags.hero"
    assert without_member("selfhost/cli/flags.hero") == "selfhost/cli/flags.hero"
    assert without_member("no colons here") == "no colons here"

    # And with the member cut, the dead one anchors on its extension — which is
    # the whole repair: it becomes visible to the check rather than skipped.
    roots: {str: bool} @ {}
    roots["selfhost"] @ true
    assert anchored(token: without_member("commands/flags.rs::FLAGS"), roots: roots)
    assert !anchored(token: "commands/flags.rs::FLAGS", roots: roots)
    assert anchored(token: without_member("selfhost/cli/flags.hero::flags()"), roots: roots)

test "the ROADMAP's § Where we are is counted to its own ceiling"
    # The real file, which is the point: this check exists because the section
    # was 31 lines against a 15-line rule nobody measured.
    r: report.Report @ report.empty()
    where_we_are(@r)
    assert r.failed == 0

    # And the ceiling is a number this file states rather than one it remembers.
    assert WHERE_CEILING == 15
